# Stateful Plugins

## Overview

You want every Turn to record who sent it, and to refuse blocked senders,
without touching any route. A stateful Plugin does that. It owns one field
in the Agent state, sees every Signal before the route runs, and updates its
field after each successful Action.

By the end of this Livebook, one Plugin adds a `ledger` field to a counter
Agent:

```text
%{count: 2, ledger: %{turns: 1, last_actor: "ada"}}
```

The counter's Actions never mention the ledger. Run the cells from top to
bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Callbacks

| Callback | Invoked | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `state_spec/1` | When Jido composes the Agent state schema | `{key, zoi_schema}` for one owned field |
| `prepare/2` | Before route selection, in `cmd/3` and on a live Server | `{:ok, input}` or `{:error, reason}` |
| `admit/3` | After `prepare/2`, on a live Server only | `{:ok, runtime_input}` or `{:error, reason}` |
| `reduce/2` | After Route state projection, before state validation | `{:ok, next_owned_value}` |

## Define the Plugin

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Ledger do
  use Jido.Plugin

  alias Jido.Agent.Plugin.{Preparation, Reduction}
  alias Jido.AgentServer.Plugin.Admission

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts) do
    {:ledger,
     Zoi.object(%{
       turns: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(0),
       last_actor: Zoi.string()
     })
     |> Zoi.default(%{turns: 0, last_actor: ""})}
  end

  @impl true
  def prepare(%Preparation{signal: %{subject: actor}}, _opts)
      when is_binary(actor) and actor != "" do
    {:ok, %{actor: actor}}
  end

  def prepare(%Preparation{}, _opts), do: {:error, :actor_required}

  @impl true
  def admit(_runtime, %Admission{prepared_input: %{actor: actor}}, opts) do
    if actor in Keyword.get(opts, :blocked, ["mallory"]),
      do: {:error, :actor_blocked},
      else: {:ok, %{admitted: actor}}
  end

  @impl true
  def reduce(%Reduction{plugin_state: ledger, prepared_input: input}, _opts) do
    {:ok, %{ledger | turns: ledger.turns + 1, last_actor: input.actor}}
  end
end
```

Each callback receives a read-only struct:

- `Jido.Agent.Plugin.Preparation`: the Signal, Agent ID, complete Agent state,
  and this Plugin's owned value.
- `Jido.AgentServer.Plugin.Admission`: the Signal, caller context, committed
  revision, owned value, and this Plugin's prepared input.
- `Jido.Agent.Plugin.Reduction`: `state_before`, `candidate_state`, the
  prepared input, and every validated Directive.

None of them can return a replacement Signal, route, context, or Agent.

## Attach It

In application code, declare the Plugin in the `plugins` block:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
state do
  schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
end

plugins do
  plugin GuidePlugins.Ledger, config: %{blocked: ["mallory", "eve"]}
end
```

This notebook uses `extensions:`, which attaches the Plugin with no options.
Use `plugin GuidePlugins.Ledger, config: %{...}` when it needs Agent-specific
config. Both forms work in a Livebook cell. `admit/3` falls back to
`["mallory"]` here.

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "ledgered_counter",
    extensions: [GuidePlugins.Ledger]

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/stateful-plugins")

    route "counter.increment", as: :increment, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      action %{amount: amount},
        name: "increment",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}), context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + amount}}
      end
    end

    route "counter.tamper", as: :tamper do
      action _input, name: "tamper", schema: Zoi.object(%{}), context: context do
        state = context.agent_state
        {:ok, %{state | ledger: %{state.ledger | turns: 99}}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

The `tamper` route tries to overwrite the ledger. You'll run it below.

## The Composed State

The Plugin's field joins the Agent schema, with its default:

```elixir
agent = GuidePlugins.Counter.instantiate!(id: "counter-1")
agent.state
```

The result is `%{count: 0, ledger: %{turns: 0, last_actor: ""}}`.

## Prepare and Reduce

The Plugin reads the actor from the Signal `subject`:

```elixir
signal = GuidePlugins.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 2}, subject: "ada")
{:ok, candidate, []} = GuidePlugins.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
candidate.state
```

The result is `%{count: 2, ledger: %{turns: 1, last_actor: "ada"}}`.
`prepare/2` returned `%{actor: "ada"}`, the Action changed `count`, and
`reduce/2` wrote the next ledger from the prepared input.

## Reject a Signal

Without a subject, `prepare/2` rejects the Signal before route selection:

```elixir
anonymous = GuidePlugins.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 2})
GuidePlugins.Counter.cmd(agent, anonymous)
```

The result is `{:error, :actor_required}`. Jido returns the Plugin's reason
as-is, not wrapped in a `Jido.Error` struct. The Action never ran.

## Owned State Is Protected

An Action can read `context.agent_state.ledger`. It can't change it:

```elixir
tamper = GuidePlugins.Counter.tamper_signal!(%{}, subject: "ada")
{:error, error} = GuidePlugins.Counter.cmd(agent, tamper)
Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"Agent executable changed Plugin-owned state"`. Only the
owning Plugin's `reduce/2` writes its field.

## Admission Is Live Only

`cmd/3` doesn't call `admit/3`, so a blocked actor gets through pure
evaluation:

```elixir
blocked = GuidePlugins.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 1}, subject: "mallory")
{:ok, candidate, []} = GuidePlugins.Counter.cmd(agent, blocked)
candidate.state.ledger.last_actor
```

The result is `"mallory"`. Put rules that must hold everywhere in
`prepare/2`. Put rules that depend on live resources, such as tokens, leases,
and rate limits, in `admit/3`.

## Run It Live

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, counter} = Jido.start_agent(GuidePlugins.Counter, id: "ledgered")

{:ok, committed, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)
rejected = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, blocked)

{committed.state.count, rejected}
```

The result is `{2, {:error, :actor_blocked}}`. The Server ran `admit/3` and
refused mallory before the Action started.

`plugin_state/2` reads the committed owned value:

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.plugin_state(counter, GuidePlugins.Ledger)
```

The result is `{:ok, %{turns: 1, last_actor: "ada"}}`. The rejected Turn
changed nothing.

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("ledgered")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- `state_spec/1` declares one owned field in the complete state schema.
- `prepare/2` runs in `cmd/3` and live. Its result is at
  `context.plugin_inputs[Plugin].prepared`.
- `admit/3` runs live only. Its result is at
  `context.plugin_inputs[Plugin].runtime`.
- `reduce/2` writes the owned field after Route state projection.
- Actions can read Plugin-owned state but can't change it.

